# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
BLOCKS = {
    "base": [
        ("Why a pet yard skips the weed barrier",
         "<p>Most residential lawns get an optional weed fabric layer between base and turf, but a {city} dog yard leaves it out on purpose. Fabric like that holds liquid against whatever's underneath it instead of letting it pass through, and a urine-soaked base that can't drain past a trapped layer of fabric turns into an odor source within one summer no matter how often the surface gets rinsed. Removing that layer means the washed rock underneath is doing the filtering job on its own, taking liquid straight down to the native sand rather than holding it at the surface where the smell lives. It's one of the few places where less material under the turf is the better build, not a shortcut.</p>"),
        ("How fast the base under a dog run has to drain",
         "<p>A dog run needs more base depth than a plain lawn, typically three to four inches of washed, open-graded crushed rock or crushed concrete instead of two to three, because liquid volume from daily rinsing and repeated urination is higher than rainfall alone on a {city} property. That deeper layer, combined with a backing built to pass over thirty inches of water an hour, has to clear a full hose rinse in well under a minute, not just eventually. A base that takes longer than that to visibly clear is too shallow or too tightly compacted for what a dog yard actually puts through it day to day.</p>"),
        ("Grading a dog run toward a drain, not just away from the house",
         "<p>A plain lawn only needs its subgrade sloped away from the foundation, but a {city} dog run gets graded toward a specific low point or drain instead, since rinse water and urine both need somewhere consistent to go rather than just somewhere away from the house. That drain point is also chosen to keep liquid moving away from a patio slab or pool deck edge, since concrete holds odor differently than turf does and a slab that stays damp near a dog area tends to stain over time. The one to two percent grade itself doesn't change, only the direction it's aimed and the reason behind picking that direction.</p>"),
        ("What a high water table does to a dog run in {city}",
         "<p>Summer in {city} pushes the water table close to the surface for weeks at a stretch, and a dog run sitting at grade on that kind of lot holds moisture from below as well as from rinsing on top. Raising the finished base slightly above the surrounding yard, using the fuller three- to four-inch depth the state's washed-rock standard allows, keeps a run above that saturation line instead of sitting in it during the wettest part of the year. Without that extra lift, a dog yard on low ground can smell and drain poorly even when the surface build above it was done correctly in every other respect.</p>"),
        ("The state's washed-subgrade rule, applied to a dog yard",
         "<p>Florida's turf standard requires subgrade material to be washed before installation so fines don't bind into a crust, and it also bars any increase in runoff leaving the property, two requirements that matter more on a dog yard than almost anywhere else on a {city} lot. Unwashed fill under a run would trap liquid at the surface the same way it traps rainwater under a lawn, except with urine added to what has to pass through. Meeting the no-runoff standard means a run's drainage point has to stay on the property rather than sending rinse water toward a neighbor's yard or the street, which is part of why the drain point gets planned before excavation starts.</p>"),
        ("Why a dog run's drain point can't aim at the street",
         "<p>A {city} corner lot often carries a drainage swale along both street frontages, and the state's rule against installing turf inside a swale rules out sending a dog run's higher liquid volume toward either one, unlike a plain lawn that sometimes has more flexibility in where its grade exits. Instead, a run on a corner lot gets graded inward, toward a point still on the property, even if that means a slightly longer routed slope than a straight shot to the nearest street-side ditch would offer. Losing the easy option doesn't change the one to two percent grade itself, only which direction it has to travel to reach a legal drain point.</p>"),
        ("Keeping a run's fence line off the septic lid",
         "<p>A {city} dog run's fenced footprint sometimes lines up with where a septic system's drainfield or pump-out lid already sits, and the state's turf rule requires that lid stay reachable regardless of what gets built around it. Planning the run's boundary to leave a gate or an unfenced gap at the lid's location, rather than enclosing it permanently inside the run, keeps a pump-out truck from needing to cut through fencing to reach it years later. This gets settled during layout, before the base is graded, since moving a fence line after rock and turf are down is a bigger job than adjusting a stake in bare soil.</p>"),
        ("Why the gate threshold needs its own firm base",
         "<p>Where a {city} dog run's gate crosses from the surrounding lawn into the fenced area, that narrow strip takes more concentrated foot and paw traffic in a day than an equivalent stretch of open turf sees in a week, since every trip in or out funnels through the same few feet. The base under that threshold benefits from an extra compaction pass beyond what the rest of the run gets, the same logic that applies to a fence-patrol path once turf is down, just addressed earlier at the rock stage instead of after wear shows up. Skipping that extra pass there is where a run's base tends to soften first.</p>"),
    ],
    "product": [
        ("Why a pet system needs a fully permeable backing",
         "<p>A plain residential lawn can use a semi-perforated backing and still meet the drainage requirement, but a {city} pet system calls for a fully permeable version instead, since liquid needs a path through the backing itself, not just around scattered perforations. That distinction matters more with a dog in the yard than the backing material's brand or thickness does, because a backing with fewer or smaller openings can pass rainwater fine while still holding urine against the surface long enough to smell. Fully permeable backing paired with the deeper rock base underneath is what actually gets liquid moving down and away rather than pooling anywhere in between.</p>"),
        ("Zeolite or coated sand: choosing infill for a dog yard",
         "<p>Two infill options do most of the odor-control work on {city} dog turf: zeolite, a natural mineral that binds ammonia as liquid passes through it, and coated silica sand, where a non-toxic coating resists bacteria growth on the sand grains themselves. Both fit the state's natural-material rule for infill, unlike rubber or other synthetic fill, which the rule reserves strictly for the ground under playground equipment. Zeolite tends to suit yards with heavier daily use since it's actively binding odor compounds, while coated sand works well for a single dog and a more typical rinse schedule, though either one needs the same permeable backing and base underneath to do its job.</p>"),
        ("Why dog turf runs shorter and denser than a lawn blend",
         "<p>Pile height for a {city} dog run typically sits at three-quarters of an inch to an inch and a quarter, shorter than the inch and a half to two inches common on a family lawn, because a lower, denser blade resists matting along the fence-line path a dog walks daily. A taller blade looks fuller on day one but tends to lay over permanently once a patrol route gets worn into it, while a shorter pile springs back after each pass. Shared yards with one occasional dog can still use a taller residential blend; a dedicated run for a determined pacer holds up better at the shorter end of that range.</p>"),
        ("Where dog turf lands in a ten- to twenty-year lifespan",
         "<p>Synthetic turf generally lasts ten to twenty years depending on UV stabilization, traffic, and infill, and a dedicated {city} dog run tends to land toward the shorter end of that window compared with a lawn the same age. Daily digging at fence lines, repeated pacing along one route, and more frequent rinsing all add wear that a family lawn without a pet simply doesn't see at the same rate. Choosing a denser, better-stabilized product for a run and keeping infill topped off both push a dog yard's realistic lifespan closer to the middle of the range instead of the low end.</p>"),
        ("What the pet turf price range covers",
         "<p>Installed pet turf runs ten to eighteen dollars a square foot, typically twelve to sixteen, running higher than a plain lawn because the backing, base depth, and infill are all upgraded specifically for a {city} dog yard rather than shared with a standard residential build. That premium isn't padding: a deeper washed-rock layer, a fully permeable backing, and zeolite or coated sand all cost more to buy and install than their lawn-grade equivalents. A homeowner comparing a lawn quote against a pet turf quote for the same square footage should expect the difference to show up specifically in those three line items, not in the visible turf blade itself.</p>"),
        ("What the manufacturer's spec sheet adds for a wet-use backing",
         "<p>A fully permeable backing built for a {city} dog run still comes with its own manufacturer spec for seam adhesive and infill rate, and following it matters more here than on a plain lawn since that backing sees far more standing moisture from daily rinsing than an ordinary residential product does. An adhesive rated for occasional dampness on a lawn install can fail faster under a pet system's near-daily wet cycle, which is why a pet-rated product's spec sheet often calls for a different bonding agent even though the visible seaming step looks identical. Following the specific product's documentation, not a general lawn install habit, is what the installed-to-specification standard actually asks for here.</p>"),
        ("Keeping odor-control infill from washing toward the drain",
         "<p>The state's rule that infill must not wash off the property applies to a {city} dog run's zeolite or coated sand exactly as it does to a plain lawn's silica, and a run graded toward one drain point concentrates rinse water enough that a lighter infill grain can travel further than it would across an open lawn's gentler runoff. Choosing a slightly heavier grain of whichever infill a run uses, and keeping the drain-point grade no steeper than it needs to be, keeps the odor-control material where it's actually doing its job instead of collecting at the low point.</p>"),
        ("Reading a pet turf quote against a lawn quote",
         "<p>A {city} pet turf quote worth comparing against a lawn bid should list backing permeability class, infill type, and base depth as separate items, since all three typically step up from a plain lawn's spec and that's where most of a pet system's added cost actually lives. A quote that just says pet turf without breaking out the deeper three- to four-inch base or the fully permeable backing leaves a homeowner unable to tell whether a lower bid is a better price or simply a lawn-grade build sold under a different name. Asking for those three lines itemized separately catches that difference before signing.</p>"),
    ],
    "process": [
        ("Grading toward the drain before the rock goes down",
         "<p>On a {city} dog run, the drain point gets located and the bare soil graded toward it before any base material arrives on site, reversing the usual order of just grading away from the house and figuring out drainage later. That sequence matters because once rock is compacted in place, correcting a drain location means tearing back out material that just went down, which costs more time than getting the grade right at the start. Strip-out and grading for a run typically take a similar amount of time as for a lawn of the same size; the difference is entirely in which direction the shovel work points.</p>"),
        ("Capping irrigation as part of a pet-yard build",
         "<p>Any sprinkler zone crossing a {city} dog run's footprint gets capped at the valve box during the base-building phase, the same step a lawn conversion goes through, since the state's rule against watering synthetic turf with an in-ground system applies to a pet area exactly the way it applies anywhere else on the lot. From that point forward, a hose handles both rinsing and any supplemental water the yard needs. This step happens early enough in the process that a crew isn't reopening finished rock work later to reach a buried line near the run.</p>"),
        ("Why seams and edges get extra attention on a dog run",
         "<p>Seams and edges take more stress on a {city} pet yard than almost anywhere else turf goes down, since a bored or anxious dog will work at a loose corner the way it never would on a family lawn nobody digs at. Seam tape and adhesive go on with the same care as any residential job, but the edge anchoring, nails set every few inches or a bender-board border, gets checked twice before the crew moves on to infill. A perimeter that would pass inspection on a lawn sometimes needs a tighter nail spacing specifically because of what a determined dog will test within the first few weeks.</p>"),
        ("Infill goes in last, chosen for what it has to control",
         "<p>Infill is the final step on a {city} dog yard build, brushed in against the grain once seams and edges are finished, and the product chosen at this stage is picked specifically for odor control rather than the ball-roll or cushioning concerns that drive infill choice on other turf projects. Zeolite or coated sand goes in at a similar rate to a standard lawn's silica sand, but the reasoning behind the choice is entirely different: which product best binds or resists whatever a dog leaves behind, not how a blade of grass looks once it's brushed upright.</p>"),
        ("Where a dog run's base meets the rest of the yard",
         "<p>Many {city} projects build a dog run and a surrounding lawn at the same time, and the two share a continuous rock base graded as one system even though the backing and infill above it change at the run's boundary. That transition line gets planned before either area is turfed, so the seam between pet-grade and lawn-grade product falls somewhere clean, along a fence line or a paver border, rather than in the middle of open grass. Building both sections together this way also means the drainage plan accounts for water crossing between the two areas instead of treating them as unrelated projects.</p>"),
        ("The hose test a dog run gets before its first use",
         "<p>Once a {city} dog run's last step of infill is brushed in, running a hose across the full footprint and watching where water goes is worth doing before a dog is ever let onto it, confirming the drain point actually clears standing water within roughly a minute the way the deeper base and permeable backing are built to. This test also surfaces any lingering odor from construction dust or adhesive before the run gets its first real use. Catching a slow spot on this walkthrough, while the crew is still on site, is simpler than tracing a smell back to a drainage issue weeks later.</p>"),
        ("Setting fence posts before the base, hanging the gate after",
         "<p>Fence posts for a {city} dog run typically go in and get set before the rock base arrives, so post holes don't disturb a freshly compacted layer, but the gate itself usually gets hung only after turf is down and the finished grade at the threshold is confirmed. Hanging a gate too early risks a hinge alignment that no longer matches the ground once turf and infill add their own thickness at the threshold. This order, posts first, gate last, keeps the run's entry point functioning correctly from day one rather than needing an adjustment once the surface is finished.</p>"),
        ("Keeping a dog off a run's base through a rainy-season build",
         "<p>Building a {city} dog run during June through September means keeping the animal off the site entirely while the base compacts, since a dog crossing freshly graded rock during or right after a storm can track mud across a section that was just leveled and undo hours of compaction work in a few minutes. A rainy-season run build typically needs a temporary barrier or a few extra supervised days compared with a dry-season job of the same size. Once turf and infill are finished, normal access resumes immediately, so the extra caution applies only to the base-building window itself.</p>"),
    ],
    "risks": [
        ("Paw safety on a hot {city} afternoon",
         "<p>Full sun pushes turf surface temperature to 120 to 150°F, occasionally higher, which is uncomfortable for bare human feet and can be worse for a dog's paw pads, especially on a run with no shade at all during peak afternoon hours. A hose rinse before playtime drops that surface temperature 30 to 50 degrees within minutes, which is the fastest correction available on a {city} run that gets full sun most of the day. Scheduling outdoor time before mid-afternoon in summer, or adding at least one shaded corner to the layout, matters as much as any product choice for keeping a dog comfortable on hot days.</p>"),
        ("Low-E glass near a fence-line dog run",
         "<p>A low-emissivity window or sliding door facing a {city} dog run can reflect enough concentrated sunlight to soften the turf directly in its path, since polyethylene blades start to give around 175 to 200°F under that kind of focused heat. A run built along a fence line under a west- or south-facing window is worth checking for this before committing to a layout, since the damage shows up as an oddly shaped scorched patch that doesn't match wear from digging or traffic. Repositioning the run a few feet, or accounting for the reflection angle in the layout, avoids the problem entirely.</p>"),
        ("Keeping a run outside a live oak's drip line",
         "<p>The same drip-line rule that applies to a lawn applies to a dog run: turf can't go inside a live oak's root zone, on the property it sits on or an adjacent one, unless a certified arborist certifies the install won't cause harm. A shaded run tucked under mature oak canopy in {city} sounds appealing for keeping a dog cooler, but the excavation for even a shallow dog-run base can damage surface roots close to the trunk. Getting the arborist's letter before digging, not after a tree starts showing stress, is the only way to use that shaded space legally and safely.</p>"),
        ("Setbacks that apply to a dog run near water",
         "<p>A {city} property backing onto a pond, canal, or conservation area still has to keep any turf, including a dog run, at least ten feet from the water's edge unless a seawall or bulkhead already separates the two, and a run can never be routed through a drainage swale even if that strip looks like the flattest, most convenient spot for it. Both limits exist because turf changes how water moves across a lot, and a dog run doesn't get an exception just because it's a smaller footprint than a full lawn. Planning the run's location around these setbacks up front avoids relocating a nearly finished project.</p>"),
        ("What an undersized base does to a dog yard by August",
         "<p>A {city} dog run built with a base too shallow to drain fast, or without the fully permeable backing a pet system needs, will hold liquid at the surface no matter how good the infill choice was on paper. The result usually isn't visible right away; it shows up as a smell that gets worse through the first full summer rather than a puddle anyone notices on installation day. By the time an owner traces the odor back to the base, the fix generally means pulling turf and adding drainage rather than just swapping infill, which is a larger repair than getting the depth right the first time would have been.</p>"),
        ("When odor infill leaves the yard during a storm",
         "<p>The state's rule against infill washing off the property applies to a {city} dog run's zeolite or coated sand as much as it does to any lawn's silica, and a run graded too steeply toward its drain point can carry a visible amount of infill past the property line during a hard rain, sometimes into a neighbor's yard downhill. That's a different failure than the run simply smelling because of a shallow base; it's a drainage and grading problem showing up as material loss rather than odor. A gentler grade at the drain point and a properly finished edge there keep infill from becoming the storm's collateral damage.</p>"),
        ("The side-street swale a corner-lot run can end up inside",
         "<p>A {city} corner lot commonly carries a drainage swale along its side-street frontage as well as its front one, and a dog run planned without checking that second boundary can end up partly inside a swale the state's rule flatly prohibits building turf inside, regardless of how convenient that stretch of yard looks for fencing. Because a run's footprint is usually smaller and more fixed by fence lines than an open lawn, discovering the conflict after fencing is already up means relocating the whole run rather than just pulling back one edge. Checking both frontages before staking the fence avoids that.</p>"),
        ("The gate threshold as a run's weakest point",
         "<p>A {city} dog run's gate threshold, the narrow strip crossed every time a dog goes in or out, takes more repeated stress in a week than a fence-line path takes in a month, and a base or seam that wasn't given extra attention there tends to show the first sign of trouble at that exact spot rather than anywhere else in the run. A dog testing a slightly soft threshold with digging or scratching can turn a minor settling issue into an open gap within days, faster than the same flaw would develop under a fence line nobody scratches at daily. That threshold is worth a closer look than the rest of the perimeter during any routine check.</p>"),
    ],
    "care": [
        ("How often a dog yard needs rinsing",
         "<p>A {city} dog run needs a hose rinse on a tighter routine than a plain family lawn, since liquid volume from daily use adds to whatever a summer storm already contributes, and letting it sit concentrates odor at the surface instead of moving it down through the base. Warm months call for the most attention, both because heat accelerates odor and because a rinse also serves as the fastest way to cool the surface before a dog goes out to play. There's no single fixed schedule that fits every household; a single dog on a small run needs less frequent attention than a multi-dog yard working the same square footage every day.</p>"),
        ("Brushing a fence-patrol path back upright",
         "<p>Dogs wear a visible route along a fence line faster than foot traffic ever wears a family lawn, and brushing that path against the grain every few weeks on a {city} run keeps the pile from matting permanently the way it would if left alone. A stiff push broom works fine for a single dog's route; a multi-dog yard with more than one worn path benefits from doing this monthly rather than a few times a year. Catching a matted section early is the difference between brushing it back upright and needing to address flattened backing later.</p>"),
        ("Watching infill levels on a dog run",
         "<p>Zeolite and coated sand infill on a {city} dog yard depletes faster than plain silica sand on a family lawn, since higher rinse frequency and heavier traffic both wash a bit more out over time. Checking infill depth every few months, rather than waiting until the run visibly looks thin, keeps the odor-control product doing its job at the concentration it was designed for. A run that's gone noticeably longer between infill top-ups than a comparable lawn is usually the first place a returning smell traces back to.</p>"),
        ("Checking a dog run's edges after a storm",
         "<p>After heavy rain or wind, a {city} dog run's perimeter is worth checking specifically, more so than a family lawn's, since a dog will find and worsen a loose edge within days where a lawn with no traffic on it might sit undisturbed for weeks. A lifted corner or a seam that's started to gap gets re-anchored quickly rather than left for the next scheduled maintenance visit, since a determined digger can turn a small gap into a much larger one in a single afternoon.</p>"),
        ("Clearing waste and debris so the base keeps draining",
         "<p>Solid waste left sitting on a {city} dog run works down into the infill layer over time rather than staying on the surface, and once it's mixed in there, the same washed-rock base built to move liquid through quickly ends up working harder to do that job. Picking it up on a routine that doesn't lag more than a day or two, especially during the busiest summer stretch, keeps the base's percolation doing what it's designed for instead of fighting through material it was never meant to filter. This is one habit that matters more on a dedicated run than almost anywhere else on the property.</p>"),
        ("What to note about a run on handoff day",
         "<p>Right when a {city} dog run's crew finishes, noting exactly where the gate threshold sits and confirming the drain point clears a hose test quickly pays off later, since both are easier to point to on day one than to relocate from memory months after the surrounding lawn has grown in around them. This is also the moment to ask which infill went in, zeolite or coated sand, since future top-ups work best matching the original product rather than mixing two different odor-control materials in the same run. A few minutes of notes on handoff day saves guesswork the first time something needs checking.</p>"),
        ("What two years of daily use shows on a dog run",
         "<p>A {city} dog run gets more concentrated daily wear than almost any other section of turf on the property, so a two-year check matters more here than on a family lawn covering the same square footage. By that point, the gate threshold has taken thousands of crossings, infill has likely thinned faster than a lawn's would from the combination of heavier rinsing and traffic, and any drainage weakness has had two full rainy seasons to reveal itself through lingering odor rather than staying theoretical. Comparing the run's current state against what it looked like at installation is the clearest way to judge whether upkeep has kept pace with actual use.</p>"),
        ("Adjusting a run's rinse routine by season",
         "<p>A {city} dog run needs the most deliberate rinsing during the dry months, since there's no rain contributing and odor concentrates faster without it, while the June-through-September rainy season shifts the useful habit toward checking the drain point after each storm rather than sticking to a fixed rinse schedule. Treating both seasons identically usually means either rinsing more than necessary during a wet stretch or letting odor build during a dry one where only a hose is doing the work rain would otherwise handle. Matching the routine to the season keeps a run from smelling worse in one part of the year than the other.</p>"),
    ],
}
